"use strict";

/* generated from story/scenes/act4_ch22.md — do not edit */

SC["a4c22_call"]={t:()=>SYS(`CHAPTER TWENTY-TWO — WEN SUYIN`)+P(`The summons comes at first light, on white paper, in a hand you haven't seen before: rounder than Madam Xian's needle-script, older, with a schoolgirl's careful loop on the final character that forty years of the Court has not trained away.`)+P(`<i>They tell me the mountain sent someone at last. Come down to the warp before the day's tension. I am easiest to talk to while I work. — W.S.</i>`)+((G.f.a4_route=="guest")?(P(`Madam Xian herself walks you down — through the third ring, onto the great spiral stair that descends inside the city's central piling, white wall on one side and black lake-water pressing the crystal on the other. She is quiet most of the way, which from her is a kind of reverence.`)):(""))+((G.f.a4_route=="spy")?(P(`Wen Duo the auditor has no business in the warp-galleries, so you go down as what the calendar calls a TENSION CLERK, borrowed robes and a tally-board, one face among the dozen that attend the morning's work. The spiral stair descends inside the central piling, white wall one side, black lake-water pressing the crystal on the other, and nobody looks at a clerk.`)):(""))+((G.f.a4_route=="prisoner")?(P(`They escort consignments below on feeding days, and today is not one — but your tag hums before dawn and two guards arrive with something new in their faces: deference, misapplied. The warp-elder has asked for the gold consignment BY NAME, and what the warp-elder asks for, the Court delivers. The spiral stair descends inside the central piling, white wall one side, black lake-water pressing the crystal on the other, and your silk tag glows faintly all the way down, like a thing coming home.`)):(""))+((G.f.a3_marked=="sho")?(P(`Granny Sho waits at the stair's mouth, cane in both hands, dressed in the same grey she has worn for forty years. She did not sleep; you can hear it in her breathing. “Boy,” she says. “However she is. Whatever they've made of her.” The cane does not tremble. “Don't let me be cruel to her for surviving.”`)):("")),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Down, to the warp.`,go:"a4c22_suyin"}]};

SC["a4c22_suyin"]={img:"suyin",
 t:()=>P(`The warp-gallery is a cathedral lit from below.`)+P(`You come out of the stair onto a balcony above a canyon of thread: the Grand Loom's upper warp, ten thousand lines of pale soul-silk rising out of the deep and passing up through the city overhead, each line under tons of tension, the whole array humming one low continuous chord that you feel in your back teeth and your healed-over grief alike. Workers move along the lines on silk gangways — tension-clerks, splicers, menders. The light comes UP, from the Loom below, the color of dawn through paper.`)+P(`She is exactly where the chord is densest.`)+P(`Wen Suyin stands in the warp the way other people stand in a doorway: half through. From the waist up she is a woman of perhaps sixty, grey-streaked, strong-handed, in working white — and her hands move across the lines with a speed and a tenderness you have never seen in any weaver including yourself. From the waist down, where her body meets the gallery floor, she is THREAD: her own soul-stuff, paid out and woven into the warp in a fan of several hundred lines, indistinguishable from the Loom except that they are, very faintly, warm-colored. Not consumed. WOVEN. A woman knitted into a machine at the hip, forty years deep, holding — you read the loads in a glance and your stomach drops — an entire province's worth of tension, alone.`)+P(`She finishes the splice under her hands before she looks up. Of course she does.`)+P(`“Gold,” she says. Her voice is silk-soft and steady. “They wrote me you were gold. I thought they meant the hair.” Her eyes find yours with no trouble at all. “Hello, stitch. You came in through my warp. I felt you on the stair — you walk like the mountain.”`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`“I came from the Azure Cloud. I have forty years of yours to deliver.”`,go:"a4c22_reunion"}]};

SC["a4c22_reunion"]={t:()=>((G.f.a3_marked=="sho")?(P(`The cane speaks first: tap, on the gallery stone, and Wen Suyin's hands stop moving on the warp for the first time since you arrived. Maybe for the first time in years.`)+P(`“That broom,” Suyin says, not turning, in the voice of a woman naming something from inside a dream. “Forty years and I'd know that gait on the far side of the sky. They told me the mountain sent SOMEONE.” Her hands come away from the thread slowly, the way you'd put down something that breaks. “They didn't say it sent the whole courtyard.”`)+P(`Granny Sho crosses the gangway alone, cane finding every plank's center, and stops a hand's width away. Two old women under a canyon of thread. The blind one reaches out, reads the air an inch from her sister's face: brow, cheekbone, the wet on it. Then, downward, to where a body becomes warp, and her fingers stop at the boundary and stay there, resting on the first woven line, very light, the whole canyon's hum running up through them.`)+P(`“You absolute fool,” Sho says, dry-leaf gentle, each word laid down like a tile. “You went and answered it. The night it took my eyes — it wasn't TAKING you. You went TOWARD the sound.” It is not a question; her hands have already read the answer in the warp. “Forty years I mourned a stolen girl. And you walked.”`)+P(`“I walked,” Suyin says. “It was tearing, little sister. Right over the courtyard. Nobody could hear it but us, and you were SCREAMING, and it was going to come down on the whole mountain.” Her thumb moves, once, across Sho's knuckles. “Somebody had to hold it. I was eldest. That was the whole arithmetic, and I have had forty years to check it, and it has never once come out different.”`)+P(`What happens then is not an embrace, because half of one of them cannot leave the loom. They stand forehead to forehead in the upward light instead, two grey heads, while the warp hums through both their hands, and you and the gallery and the Court and forty years all have the courtesy to be silent.`)):(P(`“The sweeper,” Suyin says, when you say the name, and her hands go on working but everything behind them stops. “Little Sho. They sent me her tears, you know — that's a thing the Court can do; grief carries on the lines, if it's strong, and hers crossed half a country the night I was woven in.” A splice, perfect, automatic. “Is she well. Is she — did she stay? At the mountain?” You tell her: the broom, the thousand steps, forty years of listening at the bottom of the stairs for a sound that never came again. Suyin closes her eyes. “She heard it once and it cost her the sky,” she says. “I heard it once and it cost her a sister. I have done sums for the Court forty years, stitch, and that ledger still doesn't balance anywhere I can find.”`))),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`There is a question under all of it, and you are the only one who can ask it cleanly.`,go:"a4c22_testimony"}]};

SC["a4c22_testimony"]={t:()=>P(`Qiu's training answers before you can decide how to ask: from the records outward. “The Court says you're CONTENT. The Court says a lot of things in that grammar. So: forty years. Voluntary, continuous. Why did you stay?”`)+P(`Suyin works while she answers, because she is easiest to talk to while she works, and you suspect the reverse is also true.`)+P(`“The first year I stayed because I was woven and couldn't leave. The fifth, because I'd learned the arithmetic — you've seen a tear by now; the Lady showed me the ledger of the Great Fray, the WHOLE wound, and child, it is the size of a war. The Loom holds it. Nothing else can.” A splice; a tension eased; somewhere overhead, a province's weather stays kind. “The twentieth year I stayed because I'd stopped being able to tell the warp's weight from my own. And now —” her hands open across the lines, a gesture like a shrug performed by a cathedral — “now I stay because I am LOAD-BEARING, stitch, and the woman who could walk away from a held-up sky died somewhere in year nine. The Court didn't kill her. The work did. The Court just kept the receipts.”`)+P(`She looks at you then, fully. “They'll have told you the souls are necessary. The hammock-sleepers. The fattened ones. That the Loom must eat to hold.” Her voice does not change at all, which is how it lands like a dropped beam. “Look at the warp, weaver. Look at what it's actually made of. Then come back and tell an old woman what YOU see, because I have been inside this machine for forty years, I have made my peace seventeen different ways, and I have never once been able to make it eat a person without flinching.”`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Read the warp. All of it. The full deep read she's asking for.`,req:()=>(G.insight>=13),rq:"(needs Insight 13)",do:()=>{G.f.a4_truth=true;addIns(2);},go:"a4c22_truth"},
  {id:"c1",l:`You don't have the depth for the full read yet. Ask her to show you, line by line, as Granny taught sweeping.`,do:()=>{G.f.a4_truth=true;G.f.a4_truth_taught=true;addIns(1);note("+1 Insight. It takes the rest of the day, and Suyin teaches like her sister sweeps: completely, in order, no step skipped because no step is small.");},go:"a4c22_truth"}]};

SC["a4c22_truth"]={t:()=>P(`The truth is in the tension map, and once seen it cannot be unseen.`)+P(`The warp holds because of the WOVEN lines: Suyin's hundreds, and the older, deeper fans of warp-elders before her, weavers and resonants who were knitted in alive and gave structure as she gives it — living thread under living tension, mended daily, holding because it can answer load with judgment. That is what holds the sky shut. That is ALL that holds the sky shut.`)+P(`The harvested souls — the hammock-sleepers, the fattened consignments, four hundred years of furnace tribute — go elsewhere. You trace the lines three times because the first two times you assume you've made an error. They run to the HEAT. Spun into fuel, burned to keep the warp supple, and the heat-budget is obscene: the Loom burns ninety parts in a hundred just keeping its own dead weight from going brittle. It is an engine that runs on people and spends almost all of them on running itself.`)+P(`Fuel, not structure. The Court's entire tribute economy — Kang's furnace, Clearwater's bracket, the Cocoon House, the white ledgers, four centuries of collected souls — feeds the part of the machine that does NOT hold the sky. The sky is held by the willing. It was always held by the willing. The fed souls keep the machine warm, and the machine is four hundred years of accumulated dead pattern that a better weave wouldn't need at all.`)+P(`“You see it,” Suyin says, watching your face as if a verdict were being read off it. “Say it.”`)+P(`“The lie isn't the fray. The fray is real.” Your own voice sounds far away. “The lie is the fuel. They've been burning people to heat a bad design.”`)+SYS(`THE HINGE OF THE ACT: the Loom runs on weavers, not souls. Structure is given; fuel is taken; the Court has spent four centuries conflating them. A better pattern would need no furnace at all. (a4_truth)`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Suyin's hands have stopped working. “Forty years,” she says, “I have been waiting for someone who could SEE it from outside. Sit down, stitch. We have a great deal of arithmetic to do.”`,go:"a4c22_after"}]};

SC["a4c22_after"]={t:()=>P(`You stay in the warp-gallery until the lamps change shift.`)+P(`Suyin talks design with you the way Auntie Tang talks soup: practitioner to practitioner, no mysticism, forty years of warp-craft poured out to the one person alive with hands that can hold it. Where the old pattern wastes. Where the dead weight sits. What a warp of WILLING tithe — Madam Xian's outward-running threads, multiplied, organized, freely given and freely withdrawn — could carry, if anyone could tie the center-knot that lets a pattern hold itself.`)+P(`“It can't be tied at my realm,” she says, plainly. “Nor yours, Loomheart. The old books call the knot the SAGE'S SHUTTLE and call the realm Pattern Sage and call both impossible, and the Court agrees, which is why the Lady will let you study anything you like. She is four hundred years certain that the only choices are her Loom or the falling sky.” A pause; her hands begin, absently, to mend again. “She was right, too. Right up until the river sent me a stitch with gold in his hands. Don't be careless with that, child. It's the first new number this ledger has seen in my lifetime.”`)+((G.f.a3_marked=="sho")?(P(`Granny Sho stays below when you go. From the stair you look back once: two grey heads bent together over the warp, one teaching her hands the lines, forty years of separate listening finally pointed at the same sound. You climb the rest of the way blind yourself, more or less.`)):("")),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Up, into the white city's evening, with the act's whole argument rearranged in your chest.`,go:"a4c23_descent"}]};